  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning depicts a deep upper low dropping \nsoutheast across California. This is beginning to lift Category 3 \nHurricane Polo northeast towards Baja California, where it will \nquickly weaken as it passes over the northern Mexico mountains and \nis absorbed into the upper low. Here across the Plains, upper \nridging is now centered overhead, leading to a quieter look to the \nradar compared to the past few days. Low-level easterly flow is \nkeeping some moisture and low clouds around, so can't completely \nrule out a brief sprinkle this morning. But these low clouds should \nlift north and begin to scatter out by the afternoon, allowing \ntemperatures to warm to near 80.\n\nLow-level winds will turn southerly by Tuesday as the upper low \nslowly moves east across the Southwest. Deep moisture will quickly \nadvect north as a response. A lead impulse moving east onto the \nPlains could lead to rain chances beginning by Tuesday evening \nacross central Kansas, but most of the area should stay dry through \nthe daylight hours Tuesday. Temperatures look to climb into the 80s, \nespecially across eastern KS where cloud cover stays thinner. \nTuesday night into Wednesday night, rain chances increase further as \nlow pressure associated with the remnants of Polo lift northeast \nonto the High Plains. The track of this low still looks to stay \nmostly to our west, along with the heaviest rainfall amounts, but \nmore rain still appears likely across the western half of the \nforecast area. The best heavy rain chances area-wide still look to \nbe Wednesday evening into Thursday evening. This will be as the \nupper low approaches Kansas and its moisture interacts with a cold \nfront pushing southeast across the area. Still some differences with \nwhen this front will fully pass through the area, but most model \nguidance drops off rain chances by early Friday as as cooler and \ndrier air filters in.\n\nTurning to actual impacts, not a lot of change with this forecast \npackage with regards to total rainfall amounts. Still expecting \nwidespread amounts of 1-2.5\", with a few locally higher totals of 3-\n5\". Given current hydrologic conditions, 1-3\" of rain should be \nmanageable, and wouldn't result in much notable flooding. The main \nflooding risk will tied to wherever those locally higher amounts \nfall, especially if 3-4\" can fall in a short 6 hour period. Rainfall \nrates and flash flooding potential will likely be highest along the \nboundary Wednesday PM into Thursday evening, as instability \nstrengthens some towards north-central KS with the approach of the \nupper low. On a related note, can't rule out some stronger storms \nduring this period as wind shear also increases. But the heavy rain \nshould still be the main hazard.",
